Oral pill, peak blood level: {'fasted': '0.070 mM', 'fed': '0.050 mM'} | that is 14 to 20x below the 1 mM cell dose
Cell doses: {'no effect (GSE178518, 20 ug/mL)': '0.11 mM', 'reversal (GSE226249)': '1.00 mM'}
Topical on the rabbit eye, peak tissue level at the site: {'conjunctiva': '52 mM', 'cornea': '52 mM', 'sclera': '11 mM'}
Skin gel, liposomal hydrogel, pig ear skin (PMID 26114208): flux 10.9 ug/cm2/h -> whole-dermis average if cleared by blood flow 0.024 to 0.074 mM
Skin gel, 8% nanoemulsion gel, pig skin, 404.9 ug/cm2 over 24 h (PMID 33006045): flux 16.9 ug/cm2/h -> whole-dermis average if cleared by blood flow 0.036 to 0.114 mM
Skin gel, plain pirfenidone, about 5x below the liposomal gel (PMID 26114208): flux 2.2 ug/cm2/h -> whole-dermis average if cleared by blood flow 0.005 to 0.015 mM

Neither route clearly reaches the 1 mM cell-culture level as a whole-dermis average (about 0.01 to 0.1 mM by flux / blood flow, the same order as the pill). But the cell-culture level is probably not the bar: the pill slows lung fibrosis in patients at 0.05 to 0.07 mM, 14 to 20x below it. A gel concentrates drug at the surface and in follicle openings, where the upper-follicle scarring sits (site tissue reached tens of mM on the rabbit eye), and 8% gel has clinical antifibrotic effects in human skin. The real test is a dose-response in human scalp follicles (about 0.03 to 3 mM), which the proposed organ-culture experiment should include.
